The evidence states that the Commissioner will consider requests by any interested person for scientific studies to support a color additive petition [1].  

21 CFR 70.55 requires that any request for scientific studies to support a color additive petition must:  
- Be submitted by an "interested person" [1];  
- Focus exclusively on pharmacological investigations, studies of the chemical and physical structure of the color additive, and methods of analysis of the pure color additive (including impurities) and its identification and determination in foods, drugs, or cosmetics [1];  
- Include payment of the fee prescribed under §70.19 [1].

raw:
The FDA may consider requests from a small cosmetics firm without a lab to have scientific studies conducted for a color additive petition, but such studies must meet specific criteria and be approved by the Commissioner. The available regulatory sources do not specify whether FDA will run studies for this particular entity; they only state that FDA "will consider" such requests under defined conditions [1].  

21 CFR 70.55 requires that any request for scientific studies to support a color additive petition must:  
- Be submitted by an "interested person" (which includes the described small cosmetics firm) [1];  
- Focus exclusively on pharmacological investigations, chemical/physical structure analysis of the color additive, and methods for analyzing pure color additives (including impurities) in foods, drugs, or cosmetics [1];  
- Include payment of the fee prescribed under §70.19 [1].
